      "writtenOn": "2026-11-01T19:09:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Would you mind keeping this block out of `include/linux/mm.h`? As far as I can see this would be the first P3S code in that header, with an `#ifdef CONFIG_ARM64_PER_PROCESS_PAGE_SIZE` block of its own.\n\n`p3s_vm_iomap_memory()` has a single caller and isn\u0027t exported, so it could be a `static inline` in `mm/p3s/memory.h`, next to `vma_insert_page_slices()`; the helpers there build in both configurations without stubs. `p3s_remap_phys_range()` could stay in `mm/p3s.c` while aosp/4368011 shares it (if that patch goes, as I suggest there, it could be a `static inline` as well), with its prototype next to `p3s_remap_pfn_slice()` in the existing block of `include/linux/p3s.h` and a stub in a new `#else` branch:\n\n```\n#else\nstatic inline int p3s_remap_phys_range(struct vm_area_struct *vma,\n\t\t\t\t       unsigned long addr, phys_addr_t phys,\n\t\t\t\t       unsigned long size, pgprot_t prot)\n{\n\treturn -EOPNOTSUPP;\n}\n#endif\n```\n\nThe hook in `vm_iomap_memory()` stays as it is. If you\u0027d rather keep `p3s_vm_iomap_memory()` in `mm/p3s.c`, its prototype and stub could go into that same block of `include/linux/p3s.h`. aosp/4368011 adds `p3s_mmap_phys_object()` to this `include/linux/mm.h` block too, so it could follow the same route.",
